  "author": "dvolkov",
  "body": "Nice, I've been wanting this for the ingest logs. No strong opinions on the details, I'll leave the review to Priya."
}
---
{
  "path": "logslice/cache.py",
  "line": 17,
  "author": "priya-r",
  "body": "We can't hard-code `~/.cache/logslice`: CI runners and some of our hosts have a read-only home, and it ignores the XDG spec. Please resolve the directory when it's needed (not at import time), in this order:\n\n1. `$LOGSLICE_CACHE_DIR`, if set and non-empty, used as-is (no `logslice` subdirectory appended);\n2. otherwise `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/logslice`, if `XDG_CACHE_HOME` is set and non-empty;\n3. otherwise `~/.cache/logslice`.\n\nAn explicit `directory=` argument to `load()`/`store()` should still win over all of these. Please also fix the `--cache` help text, which hard-codes the path."
}
{
  "path": "logslice/cache.py",
  "line": 34,
  "author": "priya-r",
  "body": "I don't think \"never expire\" is OK. The cache grows without bound, and our log shipper sometimes rewrites rotated files in place with the same size and mtime, so people would get stale results indefinitely. Please add a TTL:\n\n- a new CLI option `--cache-ttl SECONDS` (integer, default 3600), used together with `--cache`;\n- `load()` takes a `ttl` keyword argument (seconds, default 3600) and treats an entry whose `created_at` is more than `ttl` seconds old as a miss;\n- on a miss the CLI re-parses the file and overwrites the entry, as it already does.\n\nPlease add tests for the expiry."
}
{
  "path": "logslice/cache.py",
  "line": 21,
  "author": "priya-r",
  "body": "nit: `_key` is a bit cryptic, and I'd like to reuse it for a `logslice cache` subcommand later. Please rename it to `cache_key` (public, no underscore) and give it a docstring saying what goes into the key (resolved path, size and mtime)."
}
"
}

